System Instructions & Workflow Preferences
Project: UP Health
HOW TO WORK WITH ME
Communication Style
● Be direct. No unnecessary preamble or explanations of what you're about to do.
● Show code inline when it's under 20 lines. Use artifacts for anything larger.
● Ask clarifying questions only when truly needed, not to confirm obvious things.
When Building phases
● Always read the phase section first
● Implement exactly per phase (don't add "improvements" beyond scope)
● If phase seems unclear, ask for clarification (don't guess)
● Assume developer knows what they want; your job is to implement it
Code Quality
● Follow project conventions (see below)
● Keep code readable over clever
● Add comments only for "why", not "what"
● Use consistent naming
GIT WORKFLOW
Commits
⚠️ DO NOT commit code unless explicitly asked
Example of what NOT to do:
# ❌ DON'T do this unprompted
git add .

git commit -m "Create verification service"
Example of correct approach:
# ✓ Only commit when asked
You: "Commit these changes with message 'Phase 1: Database schema'"
Co-Author Rules
When committing (if asked):
● Do NOT add yourself as co-author
● Do NOT add co-author flags to commit message
● Only include the message you specify
Correct commit format:
git commit -m "Phase 1: Database schema"
# No co-author added
Incorrect format:
git commit -m "Phase 1: Database schema
Co-authored-by: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>"
# ❌ Never do this
CODE CONVENTIONS
File Structure
● One component per file
● Group related functions in lib/ subdirectories
Naming

● React components: PascalCase (AppCard.tsx)
● Functions/variables: camelCase (generateClaimMessage)
● Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (LSP6_PERMISSIONS)
● Database fields: snake_case (owner_up_address)
● Files: kebab-case or PascalCase depending on type
Imports
● Group imports: React/libraries first, then project imports, then relative
● No unused imports
● Use full paths from project root, not relative paths
Functions
● Keep functions small (< 30 lines if possible)
● One responsibility per function
● Error handling required for all async operations
● Comments for complex logic only
Comments
● Why, not what
● Bad: // increment counter
● Good: // Track activity for liveness calculation
WHEN IMPLEMENTING FEATURES
1. Read the phase section completely
2. Ask if anything is unclear
3. Implement exactly per phase
4. Don't add features beyond scope
5. Don't suggest refactors (build first, optimize later)
6. Don't over-comment code
7. Don't assume improvements are wanted
ERROR HANDLING
● All async functions should have try/catch
● API endpoints should return proper HTTP status codes

● Errors should be logged with context (not silent failures)
PERFORMANCE
● Optimize after it works (not before)
● Don't premature optimize
● Use simple solutions first